IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `sea-employee`

-- Seafarers Rehabilitation and Compensation Act 1992 (Commonwealth of Australia) -- the dollar amounts, from the formulas
-- the text deposit lost. Beside sea-employee.l4, which answers the weekly amount after the first 45 weeks and gives only a
-- band for the first 45; this module gives the first 45 weeks in dollars (s 31(2)), normal weekly earnings (s 13), the
-- private-car travel amount (s 28(6A)), non-economic loss (s 41) and the indexation factors (ss 23, 23A).
--
-- SEAT: the injured seafarer (or other employee under the Act), as in sea-employee.l4.
--
-- FORMULAS. Taken from ../../registers/source-bundle/C2004A04525.formulas.md (transcribed 2026-10-24 from the authorised
-- Word rendering of compilation C2025C00116, each checked against the equation image). Each rule quotes its formula in
-- that file's linear notation. Section 13 turns out to have no formula (NOTES.md finding 13).
--
-- FIGURES THAT ARE INPUTS. Nothing indexed is deposited: the CPI and WPI index numbers, the amounts in force after
-- indexation ($18,895.98 in s 41(2) and the s 31(9)-(11) figures), AWOTE and the Minister's rate per kilometre are asked
-- for.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as
-- advice. See NOTES.md (findings 12 to 17).

§ `Seafarers Rehabilitation and Compensation Act 1992 -- the amounts, from the formulas`

§§ `s 13 -- normal weekly earnings`

-- s 13 states normal weekly earnings in words, with no formula: (1) for a seafarer, "an amount equal to the amount
-- payable weekly to the employee by way of salary under the contract of employment that applied to his or her employment
-- immediately before the injury happened"; (2) for a trainee, the weekly salary "under the industrial instrument or
-- National Employment Standards that, apart from the injury, would have applied to his or her employment immediately
-- after the completion of the training course"; (3) for an employee attending a Seafarers Engagement Centre, "the amount
-- specified in an industrial instrument for the purposes of this subsection"; (4) for anyone else, the weekly salary
-- under the contract immediately before the injury. (5): increased "by the same percentage" as the weekly amount would
-- have been increased by age, service or an increment; (6): "increased or reduced by the same percentage" as the weekly
-- amount payable to the employee's class under a law or industrial instrument. The salary figure, and the percentages,
-- are the person's to state.
@export What are my normal weekly earnings under the Seafarers Act (s 13)?
GIVEN `my weekly salary that s 13 points to` IS A NUMBER @desc The weekly salary s 13 points to, in dollars: for a seafarer or other employee, your weekly salary under your contract immediately before the injury; for a trainee, the weekly salary that would have applied after the training course; at a Seafarers Engagement Centre, the amount the industrial instrument specifies
      `the percentage increase for age, service or an increment` IS A NUMBER @desc The percentage by which that weekly amount has been, or would have been, increased since the injury because you reached an age, completed a period of service or moved up a salary range (0 if none; s 13(5))
      `the percentage change for my class of employees` IS A NUMBER @desc The percentage by which the weekly amount for your class of employees has since been increased (positive) or reduced (negative) by a law or industrial instrument (0 if none; s 13(6))
GIVETH A NUMBER
`my normal weekly earnings under s 13` MEANS
    `my weekly salary that s 13 points to`
    TIMES (1 PLUS (`the percentage increase for age, service or an increment` DIVIDED BY 100))
    TIMES (1 PLUS (`the percentage change for my class of employees` DIVIDED BY 100))

§§ `s 28(6A) -- travel to treatment`

-- s 28(6A): "(a) in relation to a journey by means of public transport or ambulance services--an amount equal to the
-- expenditure reasonably incurred in undertaking that journey; or (b) in relation to a journey by means of private motor
-- vehicle--an amount worked out using the formula:
--     Specified rate per kilometre × Number of kilometres travelled
-- where: Specified rate per kilometre means such rate per kilometre as the Minister specifies, by legislative instrument
-- ...; Number of kilometres travelled means the number of whole kilometres that the employer determines to have been the
-- reasonable length of such a journey (including the return part of the journey)." Payable only where sea-employee.l4's
-- `Can I claim travel to treatment under the Seafarers Act` is met (s 28(7)). Out: s 28(6AA) (a journey from a place
-- that is not where the employee normally resides), s 28(6B) (staying there: the expenditure).
@export How much can I claim for the journey to medical treatment under the Seafarers Act (s 28(6A), (7))?
GIVEN `the travel treatment is compensable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether compensation is payable for the medical treatment you travelled to
      `I travelled by public transport or ambulance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the journey was by public transport or ambulance (rather than private car)
      `my injury reasonably required that transport` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For public transport or an ambulance: whether your injury reasonably required it
      `the expenditure I reasonably incurred on the fares` IS A NUMBER @desc For public transport or an ambulance: what you reasonably spent on the journey, in dollars
      `the reasonable length of the car journey` IS A NUMBER @desc For a private car: the reasonable length of the journey, including the return part, in kilometres
      `the specified rate per kilometre` IS A NUMBER @desc For a private car: the rate per kilometre the Minister has specified, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much for the journey to treatment under the Seafarers Act` MEANS
    IF (NOT `Can I claim travel to treatment under the Seafarers Act` `the travel treatment is compensable` `I travelled by public transport or ambulance` `my injury reasonably required that transport` `the reasonable length of the car journey`)
    THEN 0
    ELSE IF `I travelled by public transport or ambulance`
         THEN `the expenditure I reasonably incurred on the fares`
         ELSE `the specified rate per kilometre` TIMES FLOOR `the reasonable length of the car journey`

§§ `s 31 -- the weekly amount in dollars` -- arises only if the person cannot work, or not at the same rank

-- s 31(2): for "each of the first 45 weeks (whether consecutive or otherwise) during which the employee is incapacitated",
-- "an amount worked out using the formula:
--     Normal weekly earnings − Earnings in suitable employment
-- where: Normal weekly earnings means the amount of the employee's normal weekly earnings. Earnings in suitable employment
-- means the amount per week (if any) that the employee is able to earn in suitable employment." The s 31(7) cap at 150% of
-- AWOTE and the s 31(8) minimum act on amounts "worked out under subsection (5)", so neither touches the first 45 weeks.
-- A negative result (earnings in suitable employment above normal weekly earnings) is read as nil (an INFERENCE, as
-- sea-employee.l4 does for s 31(5)).
GIVEN `NWE` IS A NUMBER
      `earnings in suitable employment` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the s 31(2) amount` MEANS max 0 (`NWE` MINUS `earnings in suitable employment`)

@export How much weekly compensation do I get this week, in dollars, including the first 45 weeks (ss 31, 38)?
GIVEN `I cannot work as before this week` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, because of the injury, you cannot work at all this week, or cannot work at the same rank or level as before the injury
      `the earlier weeks counting towards the first 45` IS A NUMBER @desc How many earlier weeks of incapacity count towards the first 45 (for a seafarer, weeks after you were left on shore at, or returned to, your home port)
      `my normal weekly earnings before the injury` IS A NUMBER @desc Your normal weekly earnings before the injury, in dollars (s 13; the question above works them out)
      `what I am able to earn this week in suitable employment` IS A NUMBER @desc What your employer determines you are able to earn this week in suitable employment, in dollars (0 if none)
      `the hours I am employed this week` IS A NUMBER @desc The hours you are employed this week (0 if none)
      `my normal weekly hours of work` IS A NUMBER @desc Your normal weekly hours (ashore: those in your award, agreement or the NES, or 38)
      `I work as a seafarer at some time this week` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are employed or engaged as a seafarer for any part of this week
      `the published AWOTE` IS A NUMBER @desc The Australian Statistician's Average Weekly Ordinary Time Earnings of full-time adults, in dollars
      `the s 31(9) minimum earnings figure in force` IS A NUMBER @desc The s 31(9)(a) minimum earnings figure in force, in dollars ($254.46 in this compilation, indexed each 1 July under s 23)
      `the s 31(10) addition in force` IS A NUMBER @desc The s 31(10) addition in force, in dollars ($62.99 in this compilation, indexed)
      `the s 31(11) addition per child in force` IS A NUMBER @desc The s 31(11) addition per child in force, in dollars ($31.50 in this compilation, indexed)
      `a prescribed person is dependent on me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your spouse, or another prescribed person (an adult relative, or someone caring for your child), is wholly or mainly dependent on you
      `the prescribed children dependent on me` IS A NUMBER @desc How many prescribed children (children under 16, or students under 25) are wholly or mainly dependent on you, not counting a child already counted as the prescribed person
      `I have reached my pension age` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have reached pension age under the Social Security Act 1991
      `I was injured in the year before pension age` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were injured at or after the age one year before your pension age
      `the weeks of incapacity compensated so far` IS A NUMBER @desc How many weeks of incapacity have already been compensated for this injury (counted only for an injury within a year before pension age)
      `I am in prison for an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are in prison this week for committing an offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much weekly compensation this week in dollars` MEANS
    CONSIDER `How much weekly compensation this week` `I cannot work as before this week` `the earlier weeks counting towards the first 45` `my normal weekly earnings before the injury` `what I am able to earn this week in suitable employment` `the hours I am employed this week` `my normal weekly hours of work` `I work as a seafarer at some time this week` `the published AWOTE` `the s 31(9) minimum earnings figure in force` `the s 31(10) addition in force` `the s 31(11) addition per child in force` `a prescribed person is dependent on me` `the prescribed children dependent on me` `I have reached my pension age` `I was injured in the year before pension age` `the weeks of incapacity compensated so far` `I am in prison for an offence`
    WHEN `one of the first 45 weeks: compensation by the s 31(2) formula, which is not in the deposited text` THEN `the s 31(2) amount` `my normal weekly earnings before the injury` `what I am able to earn this week in suitable employment`
    WHEN `an amount per week after the first 45 weeks (s 31(4), (5))` `dollars` THEN `dollars`
    OTHERWISE 0

§§ `s 41 -- non-economic loss` -- arises only if the injury has left a permanent impairment

-- s 41(1): where "compensation is payable for the injury under section 39", additional compensation for non-economic
-- loss. (2): "an amount worked out using the formula:
--     [$18,895.98 × Degree of permanent impairment] + [$18,895.98 × Degree of non-economic loss]
-- where: Degree of permanent impairment means the percentage finally determined under section 39 ...; Degree of
-- non-economic loss means the percentage determined under the approved Guide, by the employer". The amount is a s 23
-- relevant amount, indexed: the figure in force is an input, used for both terms. Whether s 39 compensation is payable is
-- worked out with sea-employee.l4's threshold (a maximum amount of 1 is passed there: only whether the result is above nil
-- is read).
@export How much compensation for non-economic loss do I get under the Seafarers Act (s 41)?
GIVEN `it is a lost finger, toe, taste or smell` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the impairment is the loss, or loss of use, of a finger or toe, or the loss of the sense of taste or smell
      `my degree of permanent impairment` IS A NUMBER @desc The degree of permanent impairment finally determined under s 39, as a percentage (for example 20 for 20%)
      `my degree of non-economic loss` IS A NUMBER @desc The degree of non-economic loss your employer determined under the approved Guide, as a percentage
      `the s 41(2) amount in force` IS A NUMBER @desc The s 41(2) amount in force, in dollars ($18,895.98 in this compilation, indexed each 1 July by the CPI under s 23)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much for non-economic loss under the Seafarers Act` MEANS
    IF `How much permanent impairment compensation under the Seafarers Act` `it is a lost finger, toe, taste or smell` `my degree of permanent impairment` 1 GREATER THAN 0
    THEN (`the s 41(2) amount in force` TIMES `my degree of permanent impairment` DIVIDED BY 100)
         PLUS (`the s 41(2) amount in force` TIMES `my degree of non-economic loss` DIVIDED BY 100)
    ELSE 0

§§ `ss 23, 23A -- indexation of the amounts`

-- s 23(5): the CPI factor is "the number (calculated to 3 decimal places) worked out by dividing the index number of the
-- December quarter immediately before the relevant year by the index number for the December quarter immediately before
-- that first-mentioned December quarter"; (6): if it "would, if it were calculated to 4 decimal places, end with a number
-- greater than 4", increased by 0.001.
-- s 23A(3): the WPI factor is "the number calculated, to 3 decimal places, using the formula:
--     Index number for the reference December quarter / Index number for the base December quarter"
-- (the reference December quarter the last before the relevant year, the base the one before that); (4): the same 0.001
-- rule. "Calculated to 3 decimal places" is read as cutting off after the third place (an INFERENCE).
GIVEN `the later index number` IS A NUMBER
      `the earlier index number` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the Seafarers Act indexation factor` MEANS
    IF `fourth place` GREATER THAN 4 THEN `three places` PLUS 0.001 ELSE `three places`
    WHERE
        `ratio`        MEANS `the later index number` DIVIDED BY `the earlier index number`
        `three places` MEANS FLOOR (`ratio` TIMES 1000) DIVIDED BY 1000
        `fourth place` MEANS FLOOR (`ratio` TIMES 10000) MINUS (FLOOR (`ratio` TIMES 1000) TIMES 10)

-- s 23(4), s 23A(2): if the factor "is greater than one, this Act has effect as if for each relevant amount there were
-- substituted, on the first day of that relevant year, an amount worked out by multiplying by that factor" the relevant
-- amount or "the last substituted amount". s 23's relevant amounts: ss 30(2), 31(9), (10), (11), 39(9), 41(2), 43(2),
-- (4), 44(1); s 23A's: s 29(3), (4), (5). No rounding of the amount is prescribed.
DECLARE `Which Seafarers Act index` IS ONE OF
    `the Consumer Price Index (s 23: the amounts in ss 30(2), 31(9) to (11), 39(9), 41(2), 43(2), (4), 44(1))`
    `the Wage Price Index (s 23A: the amounts in s 29(3) to (5))`

@export What does a Seafarers Act amount become on 1 July after a year's indexation (ss 23, 23A)?
GIVEN `the index` IS A `Which Seafarers Act index` @desc Which index the amount moves with
      `the amount before indexation` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount in the Act, or the amount last substituted for it by indexation, in dollars
      `the index number for the last December quarter before the year` IS A NUMBER @desc The index number (All Groups CPI, weighted average of the 8 capital cities; or the Wage Price Index, total hourly rates of pay excluding bonuses, all sectors, all Australia, original) for the December quarter just before the 1 July
      `the index number for the December quarter a year earlier` IS A NUMBER @desc The same index number for the December quarter a year before that
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the Seafarers Act amount after a year's indexation` MEANS
    IF `the factor` GREATER THAN 1
    THEN `the amount before indexation` TIMES `the factor`
    ELSE `the amount before indexation`
    WHERE
        `the factor` MEANS `the Seafarers Act indexation factor` `the index number for the last December quarter before the year` `the index number for the December quarter a year earlier`
